<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · sea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gulf of St. Lawrence</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Gulf of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-lawrence-river">St. Lawrence</a> is a large inlet of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-ocean">Atlantic Ocean</a> in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> at the mouth of the St. Lawrence River. French explorers, notably <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacques-cartier">Jacques Cartier</a>, entered it in the sixteenth century. It served as the gateway to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-france">New France</a> and the North American <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">fur trade</a> in the foundation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-colonial-empire">French Empire</a> overseas.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gulf-of-st-lawrence.jpg" alt="Gulf of St. Lawrence" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nichole Ouellette/ouellette001.com; -67.367292 · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Baie_Trinite_023.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Gulf of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-lawrence-river">St. Lawrence</a> connects the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-lakes">Great Lakes</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-ocean">Atlantic Ocean</a> by way of the St. Lawrence River. It is bounded on the north by the Labrador Peninsula and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a>, on the east by Saint-Pierre and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newfoundland">Newfoundland</a>, on the south by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nova-scotia">Nova Scotia</a> peninsula and Cape Breton Island, and on the west by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaspe-peninsula">Gaspé Peninsula</a>, New Brunswick and Quebec. Half of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>'s ten provinces adjoin it: New Brunswick, Nova Scotia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-edward-island">Prince Edward Island</a>, Newfoundland and Labrador, and Quebec. It contains many islands, among them Anticosti, Prince Edward Island, Cape Breton, Miquelon-Langlade and the Îles-de-la-Madeleine.</p><p>There is no consensus on where the river ends and the gulf begins, or whether it is hydrographically a gulf or an estuary. Geographers since <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samuel-de-champlain">Samuel de Champlain</a> have treated Pointe des Monts as the dividing point, and a lighthouse was built there in 1829–1830. The waters leave the gulf by several straits: the Jacques Cartier Strait, the Honguedo Strait, the Strait of Belle Isle between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/labrador">Labrador</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newfoundland">Newfoundland</a>, and the Cabot Strait, about 56 miles wide, between Cape Ray and Cape North. The Laurentian Channel, formed in earlier ice ages by the erosion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-lawrence-river">St. Lawrence River</a> when sea level fell, is about 290 m deep and about 1,250 km long.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The gulf has long been an important fishery for the First Nations who have lived on its shores for millennia and used its waters for transport. Basque whalers from Saint-Jean-de-Luz entered it in 1530 and began whaling at Red Bay, on the Strait of Belle Isle, where they worked closely with the Iroquois. In 1579 the English government closed all English ports to Spanish oil imports, so that a third of Basque whale oil could not be sold, and Basque whaling in the gulf collapsed and never recovered.</p><p>The French explorer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacques-cartier">Jacques Cartier</a> made a documented voyage in its waters in 1534. He named the shores of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-lawrence-river">St. Lawrence River</a> the Country of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canadas</a>, after an indigenous word meaning village or settlement, and the word became the name of Canada. In winter large quantities of ice form in the river and drift towards the gulf, which is covered by ice in January and February. The island of Saint Paul, off Cape Breton Island, is known as the Graveyard of the Gulf for its many shipwrecks.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gulf of St. Lawrence” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Jacques Cartier reached the <b>Gulf of St. Lawrence</b> in 1534, claiming territory for Francis I, but decades of religious civil war prevented meaningful colonisation.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-empire">French Empire · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
